--- machine\system\network\tcpip\portreservations
canonical: Machine\System\Network\TcpIp\PortReservations

Port reservations: who may claim a TCP or UDP port. Every value under
this key is one reservation — its **name** is a selector naming a
protocol set and a port range, its **data** is a self-relative security
descriptor — and the key's unnamed default value (`@`) is the default
reservation, consulted when no selector contains the port being bound.

At `bind(2)` to a non-zero port, KACS finds the most specific selector
containing `(protocol, port)` — the one covering the fewest ports —
and evaluates the caller's effective token against that descriptor for
`PORT_BIND` (`0x00000001`). Binding port 0 (ephemeral allocation) is
never checked. `CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE` is in the always-allow set, so the
Linux privileged-port floor never refuses a bind first: this key decides
for every port, including those below 1024.

**Selector grammar.** `<proto>[,<proto>]:<lo>[-<hi>]` — `proto` is
`tcp`, `udp` or `*` (both), case-insensitive; ports are decimal
1–65535 with no leading zeros; `lo <= hi`. Examples: `tcp:80`,
`udp:53`, `tcp,udp:1-1023`, `*:8080`. A protocol may not repeat, so
every selector has exactly one spelling. The address family is
deliberately absent: one reservation covers IPv4 and IPv6, or dual-stack
squatting would bypass it.

**Descriptor.** A port descriptor carries only `PORT_BIND` and
`READ_CONTROL`. **Editing** a reservation is a write to this key,
governed by the key's own descriptor; `WRITE_DAC` or `WRITE_OWNER`
inside a port descriptor mean nothing and are never honoured. Write with
`sd set` against the value, or ship as a `.reg` seed.

**The table is loaded whole and rejected whole.** The kernel reads every
value under this key, validates all of them, and swaps the new table in
atomically. If any name is malformed, any descriptor does not parse, the
default is missing or duplicated, or two selectors of equal width
overlap (so no most-specific match would exist), the load is rejected
with an audit event and the previous table stays in force. Nested and
differing-width overlap is fine — it is how specificity works. The
registry still shows what was written; the audit log shows what the
kernel is using.

**Before the registry.** Until the first successful load — during early
boot, or if the registry is unavailable — a compiled-in table answers:
`@` grants `PORT_BIND` to SYSTEM and nobody else. It is replaced, never
merged, by the first load.

**Rebinding.** Binding onto a port that is already bound (with
`SO_REUSEADDR` / `SO_REUSEPORT`) is still a `bind(2)` and still meets
this check; the kernel additionally records the permitted binder on the
socket for the rebind rule (same user SID, or SeTcbPrivilege).

Security on this key: inherits the Machine hive root descriptor (SYSTEM
and Administrators: KEY_ALL_ACCESS; Authenticated Users: KEY_READ).
Reading reservations is harmless; writing them is administration.

--- machine\system\network\tcpip\portreservations @
canonical: Machine\System\Network\TcpIp\PortReservations @
type: REG_BINARY
default: (shipped seed) Everyone: PORT_BIND
applies: live

The default reservation: the descriptor governing every port no
selector contains. The shipped seed grants `PORT_BIND` to Everyone —
an unreserved port is anyone's to claim, as on Windows. Absent this
value the whole table is rejected and the previous table (or the
compiled-in SYSTEM-only default) stays in force.
